 "result": "**Result:** 24 failed, 1 error, 462 passed, 1 skipped.\n\n**Main root cause:** `src/tessel/sessions.py:374` passes a `partitioned=` keyword to `response.set_cookie()` (and to `delete_cookie()`), but the installed Werkzeug's `Response.set_cookie()` doesn't accept that argument. It raises `TypeError: Response.set_cookie() got an unexpected keyword argument 'partitioned'`. This is a version mismatch: the code needs a newer Werkzeug (the `partitioned` cookie attribute arrived in Werkzeug 3.1) than the one installed.\n\nAbout 20 of the 24 failures are this TypeError, in any test that writes a session cookie. That covers the session tests in `test_basic.py`, `test_extended_flashing`, `test_flash_signal`, `test_standard_context`, `test_redirect_session`, `test_session_transactions`, `test_session_transaction_ipv6`, `test_copy_context_thread`, and `test_stream_keeps_session`. The error in `test_stream_keeps_session` is likely a fixture teardown side effect of the same failure.\n\n**Likely separate failures (not investigated in detail):**\n- `test_helpers.py::TestStreaming::test_async_view`: a `RuntimeError`, probably a missing async dependency.\n- `test_helpers.py::test_open_resource_exceptions[a]`: a `pytest.Pytest...` error.\n- `test_reqctx.py::test_session_dynamic_cookie_name`: an `AssertionError`.\n- `test_request.py::test_limit_config`: `assert None...`.\n\nI haven't confirmed the installed Werkzeug version or the causes of these four, and I changed nothing.",
